  "text": "**Solution 3 is the only candidate that survives, though its upper bound is not fully verified.**\n\n**Solutions 1 and 2 are wrong because n = 8 is a counterexample.** Take H = SQS(8), the 14 planes of AG(3,2) as 4-edges. Every triple lies in exactly one plane, so δ₃(H) = 1. Any two planes share at most 2 points, so each 5-set contains at most one plane. There are 14·4 = 56 incidences and C(8,5) = 56 five-sets, so every 5-set contains exactly one plane B. Let F = B ∪ {w} and take any cyclic order on F. The two consecutive triples avoiding w both lie in B, and each is covered only by B. They would need two distinct edges, so no Berge-C₅³ exists on F. Since every 5-set has this property, H has no Berge-C₅³ at all, and 5 ≤ 8. So n = 8 fails, which rules out n₀ = 6 and n₀ = 7.\n\n**Solution 3's lower bound holds.** n₀ ≥ 9 follows from the SQS(8) counterexample. Its claim that every n ≥ 9 works is plausible: the obstruction only arises from the rigid SQS(8) structure, and a rough counting argument suggests it cannot persist at n = 9. I checked some cases, such as adding a vertex 9 with 3-edges to SQS(8), and found they still contain Berge-C₅³. I did not verify all t and all n ≥ 9, so the value 9 is the best-supported answer rather than a proven one.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
